msgid "**Search allocation precheck:** [search_allocation_precheck.sh](files/shell/search_allocation_precheck.sh) creates a one-shard probe index named after the container and fails with the answer of `_cluster/allocation/explain`, which names the disk watermark when the data disk is too full, unless Elasticsearch allocates it. `init` runs it in front of `zammad-init`. In compose, the one-shot `precheck` service runs it first and `init` depends on that service, so `compose up` stops on a refusal. Swarm renders no `precheck` service, because `depends_on` does not gate `init` there. Instead the deploy waits until the newest `init` task is `Complete`: a refusing Elasticsearch leaves it `Failed`, the wait stops the deploy, and `docker service logs` of `init` carries the explanation. An `init` the stack deploy did not restart is already `Complete` and passes at once."
msgstr ""

msgid "**Search allocation precheck:** `search_allocation_precheck.sh <files/shell/search_allocation_precheck.sh>`__ creates a one-shard probe index named after the container and fails with the answer of ``_cluster/allocation/explain``, which names the disk watermark when the data disk is too full, unless Elasticsearch allocates it. ``init`` runs it in front of ``zammad-init``. In compose, the one-shot ``precheck`` service runs it first and ``init`` depends on that service, so ``compose up`` stops on a refusal. Swarm renders no ``precheck`` service, because ``depends_on`` does not gate ``init`` there. Instead the deploy waits until the newest ``init`` task is ``Complete``: a refusing Elasticsearch leaves it ``Failed``, the wait stops the deploy, and ``docker service logs`` of ``init`` carries the explanation. An ``init`` the stack deploy did not restart is already ``Complete`` and passes at once."
msgstr ""

